# DART text_to_obs Program
# this file was generated with AI tool so take care
# The fortran code served as input to the file

## Overview

The `text_to_obs.f90` program is a utility for converting text-based observation data into DART's observation sequence file format (`obs_seq.out`). This program is part of the Data Assimilation Research Testbed (DART) framework developed by UCAR.

This specific implementation is designed to read IMS (Integrated Multisatellite Retrievals) observation data from a text file and convert it to DART's native observation format. The program handles various observation types commonly found in meteorological applications.

## Supported Observation Types

The program currently supports the following observation types:

- Temperature at 2m (type 40: `TEMPERATURE_2M`)
- U-component of wind at 10m (type 41: `U_WIND_10M`)
- V-component of wind at 10m (type 42: `V_WIND_10M`)
- Surface pressure (type 43: `SURFACE_PRESSURE`)
- Land surface relative humidity (type 83: `LAND_SFC_RELATIVE_HUMIDITY`)

## Input File Format

The input text file should have space-separated values with each line containing an observation. The expected format for each line is:

```
<obs_type> <lat> <lon> <vert> <year> <month> <day> <hour> <minute> <second> <value> <error>
```

Where:
- `obs_type`: Integer code for the observation type (40, 41, 42, 43, or 83)
- `lat`: Latitude in degrees (-90 to 90)
- `lon`: Longitude in degrees (0 to 360)
- `vert`: Vertical coordinate value (surface observations use 0, or the heigt above sea level)
- `year`, `month`, `day`, `hour`, `minute`, `second`: Time of observation
- `value`: The actual observation value
- `error`: Observation error (standard deviation)

## Configuration

The program uses a namelist file (`input.nml`) to specify runtime options. The relevant namelist section is `text_to_obs_nml` with the following parameters:

```fortran
&text_to_obs_nml
   text_input_file = '../data/text_input_file'  ! Path to input text file
   obs_out_file    = 'obs_seq.out'             ! Path for output file
   debug           = .false.                    ! Enable debug printing
/
```

## Usage

1. Prepare your IMS observation data in the required text format
2. Modify the `input.nml` file to specify your input and output files
3. Compile the program (if needed) using the DART build system
4. Run the program:
   ```bash
   ./text_to_obs
   ```
5. The program will create a DART observation sequence file named according to your `obs_out_file` setting (default: `obs_seq.out`)

## Example

For a temperature observation at latitude 40.5, longitude 255.0, at the surface (0.0), taken on 2023-01-15 at 12:30:00 UTC, with a value of 278.2 Kelvin and an error of 1.5:

```
40 40.5 255.0 0.0 2023 1 15 12 30 0 278.2 1.5
```

## Notes

- The program assumes the GREGORIAN calendar type
- Longitudes must be in the range 0-360 degrees
- For surface pressure observations, the input is assumed to be in hPa/mb (the program will convert to Pa if needed)
- Quality control values are set to 0.0 (good data) by default
- The program is set up to handle a maximum of 100,000 observations, but this can be modified in the source code if needed

## Error Handling

- The program will skip observations with invalid latitude or longitude values
- Unsupported observation types will be reported and skipped
- The program will terminate processing when it reaches the end of the input file or encounters an unrecoverable read error

